  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe heat and humidity will begin to ramp up with the start of \nanother heat wave through all of this week as a 594 dm ridge of high \npressure builds over the FA today. Despite an increase in southerly \nreturn flow, moisture advection will lag for another day as \ndewpoints will once again mix down into the lower 60s this \nafternoon. Given 850 mb WAA and deep layer mixing, nudged NBM maxTs \nup today into the mid 90s. By Monday, the stout ridge will be parked \nover the FA, with dewpoints ranging from the upper 60s to lower\n70s. Due to the modest increase in moisture, temps are less \nlikely to overachieve heading into the middle of the week. The \nNBM distribution is pretty tight through Thursday, falling in \nline with the 2m temps on the AI GFS/EC for daily highs in the \nmid to upper 90s. This translates to daily heat index values of \n100+ degrees.\n\nAfter collaborating with our WFO neighbors, have decided to hold off \non issuing any heat headlines as Monday looks to be pretty \nborderline compared to Tuesday-Friday when the LREF supports a 70-\n90% chance of needing a Heat Advisory. Unlike the previous heat \nwave, dewpoints really struggle to rise above 70 degrees making it \ndifficult for heat index values to reach extreme levels. In fact, \nthe LREF only supports a 15-30% chance of values exceeding 105 \ndegrees as it would take maxTs trending higher into the triple \ndigits to achieve these levels. The Heat Risk product may also be \nrunning a bit \"hot\" compared to the impacts that are likely as it \ndoes not factor in the previous long duration heat waves that \noccurred well into this summer with much higher dewpoints and \nsimilar temperatures. Overall, major heat impacts from the long \nduration can be expected as a Heat Advisory will be needed by early \nnext week for what will be a much drier heat. \n\nLimited relief begins to arrive by next weekend, but the \nGFS and ECMWF differ on how quickly the ridge breaks down. NBM PoPs \nhave now increased to a 20-30% chance for isolated showers and \nthunderstorms on Friday and Saturday as a frontal boundary stalls \nsouth of the Great Lakes region. Some of the model ensembles suggest \nthere is still room for rain chances to trend a bit higher, \nespecially over northeast portions of the region. Timing is \nuncertain as it is contingent on how quickly the ridge breaks \ndown.",
